            "summary": "Take-home: choose one useful next step; you do not have to eliminate worry to deserve support.\n\nThe raw total sums the 24 response scores and ranges from 24 to 96. A larger total means that, overall, these exam-related worries, self-doubts and perceived difficulties were described as more typical of you. It is not a count of worrying episodes or a test of intelligence. The form does not set a fixed number of days to recall.\n\nFor example, thoughts about failing might interrupt revision, or attention might shift to how others seem to be doing during a test. These are reflection prompts, not findings about your answers. The same total can come from different experiences; it cannot identify your most difficult phase or predict your grade. Preparation conditions, sleep, the importance of an exam and available support are useful context to consider; the score does not tell you what caused the experience.\n\nKeep arrangements that already help. If a situation feels difficult, look for one manageable action or one practical source of support, rather than forcing optimism or blaming yourself. There is no low, moderate or high label here, and no score you must reach before a concern deserves attention.\n\n中文对照：带走一句话：先选一个有用的下一步，不必消除担心才值得获得支持。\n\n总分是24题相加，范围24–96；越大表示这次整体更觉得考试担忧、自我怀疑及相关困难的描述符合自己，不是担忧发生次数或智力测验。原题没有固定最近几天的回顾窗口。\n\n例如，想到可能考不好而打断复习，或考试时注意到别人做得怎样，只是回顾提示，不是系统已判断你的作答模式。相同总分可来自不同经历，不能确定哪个阶段最困难，也不能预测成绩。准备条件、睡眠、考试重要性和支持值得一起看，但不是分数已经查明的原因。\n\n已有好用的安排可以保留；遇到困难时，可以找一个可行行动或现实支持，不强迫乐观，也不全怪自己。这里不分低、中、高，不需要达到某个数字，困扰才算值得被认真对待。",
            "bands": [],
            "guidance": [
              {
                "category": "reflection",
                "text": "Recall one ordinary moment before, during or after a test. Separate the thought that appeared from one manageable action, such as checking a concept or asking a clear question. Notice what support or condition would help; you do not have to replace worry with a positive statement first. / 只回顾一次考前、考中或考后的普通片段，把出现的担心与一个可做动作分开，如核对一个概念或问清一个问题。也看看缺少什么条件或支持，不必先把担心改成积极的话。"
              },
              {
                "category": "strategy",
                "text": "In low-pressure practice, choose one restart step for getting stuck: identify the question, note what you know and, only where rules allow, decide whether to move on and return. Alternatively, after a test, record one verifiable adjustment and then return to rest or daily life. Choose what fits; neither step guarantees a result or requires perfect calm. / 在低压力练习里，只选一个卡住后的接续动作：看清问题、写出已知条件，并仅在规则允许时决定先往下做再回来。也可以改选考后记下一项可核实的调整，再回到休息或日常生活。不必全做，也不保证成绩或要求立刻平静。"
              },
              {
                "category": "support",
                "text": "If exam-related worry keeps disrupting sleep, preparation or daily life, discuss one concrete example with a trusted educator, learning-support professional or qualified mental-health professional. Choose a safe, appropriate person; no score threshold is needed, and asking for help is not evidence of poor effort. / 若考试担忧持续影响睡眠、准备或日常生活，可把一个具体例子带给可信的教育工作者、学习支持人员或合适心理健康专业人士讨论。选择安全、合适的对象，不必达到分数线，也不表示不够努力。"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "limitations": [
          "Adult research candidate, mainly university evidence; self-description only—not diagnosis, ability, grade prediction or severity grading. / 仅成人研究候选，证据主要来自大学情境；自述非诊断、能力、成绩预测或严重分级。"
        ],
